Interoperability and non-affiliation statement
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ripcord is an independent interoperable client. It is NOT affiliated with,
authorised by, endorsed by, or sponsored by Sony Interactive Entertainment Inc.
or any of its affiliates.

"PlayStation", "PS5", "PS4", "DualSense", "DualShock" and "Remote Play" are
trademarks or registered trademarks of Sony Interactive Entertainment Inc.
Ripcord uses these names only where necessary to describe compatibility
truthfully (nominative use). No Sony trademark, logo, or other brand asset is
included in this repository or in any build produced from it.

Ripcord's protocol specification was derived independently, by static analysis
of the publicly distributed vendor client running on hardware the authors own,
and by capturing the authors' own network traffic between their own client and
their own console under their own account. It reproduces interface facts
required for interoperability -- protocol field numbers, byte offsets, enum
values, JSON keys and HTTP header names -- and does not reproduce vendor source
code or other authored expression. Vendor code is referenced only by relative
virtual address.

Interoperability constants
--------------------------

Ripcord includes a small set of constants (approximately 3 KB: four key-derivation
tables — a PlayStation 5 pair and a PlayStation 4 pair — four field context keys,
one key table, one transform table, and a byte offset) that the console itself
computes against. A client cannot speak this protocol without them, and they
cannot be altered without breaking interoperability, so they are interface facts
rather than authored expression.
They are included as data, read at runtime through the same configuration seam
that accepts a local override, and are not compiled into program logic.

These values are generic to the protocol. They are identical for every user and
every console. Ripcord includes NO key material, credential, or identifier tied
to any particular console, user, or account: no registration key, no pairing
record, no session key, no device or account identifier. Those are generated or
captured per user and stay on the user's own machine.

A build that omits the constants entirely can be produced with
-p:BundleInteropConstants=false; the result is inert and reports the constants as
unavailable rather than failing at run time.

No vendor source code is included in any form. See docs/protocol/README.md for
the full provenance writeup.

